Holds the render deadline's budget through misses that come close
together at different budgets. A miss within one more hold's length
after the hold ended now doubles the hold, as one at the same budget
always did, so a need that wanders as the GPU's other work comes and
goes no longer resets the hold to a second at each miss and lets the
budget decay into the next. Within the hold, a miss at its floor doubles
it as before, and the climb that follows is held for about a minute,
longer for one that levels off just above the floor its last miss
left, so a burst that meets a hold at its floor doesn't keep its
budget for the ten minutes a steady need's hold grows to. A burst soon
after a hold ended can't be told from a need that came back, so it
keeps the doubled hold: up to ten minutes of a larger budget, a few
milliseconds of the deadline's gain but no misses. On Joel's own GPU,
on a 60 Hz screen, the deadline missed 3,832 vblanks in its first 2.5
hours after a boot (20 November), one in at most 51 deferred frames,
each shown a refresh late. In the tests' model, a need of 3 ms and
6 ms by turns every 7 s missed 1364 vblanks in half an hour and now
misses 52; one jittering from 2.5 to 5.5 ms missed 338 in ten minutes
and now misses 27, each with a mean budget under 0.1 ms over the
larger need. A half-second burst once the hold has grown costs a mean
budget of 3.15 ms over the half hour, where keeping the hold it came
in cost 3.54 with five minutes of that hold still to run. A steady
need, one slow frame within it, or a GPU too slow for the deadline,
misses as many as before. Ours, made on the rest.

